          "title": "You'll want to know: नाक",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "नाक (nāk) — \"a nose\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "From Sanskrit nāsikā, 'nostril', by way of Prakrit — and nāsikā is a cousin of Latin nāsus and of English \"nose\", which makes this one of the clearest family resemblances anywhere on the body."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Hindi puts the nose where English puts the face: to have your नाक (nāk) cut is to be humiliated, and to keep it high is to keep your honour. The idiom is old enough that the literal sense has stopped showing through."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Four, and the last one is on the inside."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
